verifiers.reason:
I could not refute the grade. I read all three papers at source myself.

SOURCES MATCH THE AUDIT'S QUOTES. Poisson-Visser gr-qc/9506083v1, all 4 pages: eqs (6), (8), (11)-(13), (15), (17), (23), (27), (33)-(36), the 'perturbations can grow (at least until the nonlinear regime is reached)' sentence and the sec. IV caveat that beta0 need not be the sound speed are all as quoted. PV treat only the equal-mass Schwarzschild wormhole with a > 2M. So the audit's ATTRIBUTION DISCREPANCY is real: the two-mass V named 'poisson_visser' at linstab.py:507 and 'Poisson-Visser: ... V(R) = ...' at stability.py:40 is not in PV. It is a naming discrepancy, not a mathematical one. Ishak-Lake gr-qc/0108058v2 is also as quoted:
- eq (5), Rdot^2 = eps + ([m]/M)^2 - 2 eps mbar/R + (M/2R)^2, 'a quadratic identity [that] applies to both shells and wormholes', credited to Lake 1979;
- sec. II.B-C: transparency, with static embeddings required;
- sec. II.D: Upsilon != 0, and P'/sigma' may be negative;
- sec. III.B / Fig. 2: 'R/m+ ~ 2.37 as m- -> 0 and ~ 3 as m- -> m+' at P'/sigma' = 1.

INDEPENDENT ALGEBRA. With eps = -1, the tree's V = 1 - 2Mo/R - ((Mo-Mi)/ms - ms/2R)^2 expands to Rdot^2 = -1 + ([m]/ms)^2 + (Mo+Mi)/R + (ms/2R)^2. That is IL eq (5), since 2 mbar/R = (Mo+Mi)/R. The tree's sigma0 and p0 are the PV eq (8) Lanczos values, using K^tau_tau = (Rddot + M/R^2)/sqrt(f+Rdot^2). The second derivative m2 = -8 pi p + 16 pi beta^2 (sigma+p) follows from m_s' = -8 pi R p, p' = beta^2 sigma' and sigma' = -2(sigma+p)/R. The table values in stability.py also match the closed form:
- beta2_crit = -0.00368 at m/R = 0.01 and -0.1326 at m/R = 1;
- V''(beta^2 = 0) = +2.966e-2 for the device and -3.035e-2 for the ordinary shell;
- sigma = 7.918e-4 and p = -1.9504e-6 at m/R = 0.01.

INDEPENDENT NUMERIC ROUTE. My script is verify/refute/ilpv_indep.py, written without the squared potential or the auditor's script. It solves the conservation law plus the linear EOS exactly for sigma(R). It then solves the UNSQUARED Israel condition sqrt(f_in+X) - sqrt(f_out+X) = m_s/R for X = Rdot^2 by root-finding, and finite-differences V = -X at 40 digits. At m/R = 0.01, 0.5 and 2 I set beta^2 = beta2_crit -/+ 0.01. V(R0) comes out ~1e-40 in every case, V'' matches the closed form to 7 significant figures, and its sign flips exactly at beta2_crit. Because the root-finding solved the UNSQUARED condition, this route checks the dropped orientation hypothesis directly rather than assuming it. I also re-ran the auditor's script: 24/24 pass.

LATER LITERATURE, NOW READ. Pitre-Schneider-Poisson arXiv:2604.05980 was readable through alphaXiv this time, so it is no longer NAMED-NOT-READ:
- its configuration is Minkowski inside and Schwarzschild outside, with a barotropic EOS;
- eq (2.7) is dp = Gamma p/(mu+p) dmu, which gives Gamma = beta^2 (mu+p)/p, as wall.py states;
- eq (3.16c) is Gamma_1 = (4 - 6C + 2 sqrt(1-2C))/(4(1-2C)), matching wall.py's quotation, so the auditor's check 5g rests on a now-verified formula;
- the l >= 2 even-parity growing mode holds 'for all sampled values', and the plots sample M/R <= 0.3 and Gamma in [1.8, 2.4].

This narrows what 'stable' can mean beyond l = 0. It does not touch the radial result as the tree uses it: D26_CLAIM, stability.py and linstab.py:211-219 all scope the claim to the classical radial sector and keep l >= 1 NOT-RUN.

WHY NOT NARROWED. The two hypotheses the tree does not name are:
- the orientation sign conditions lost on squaring. Both are positive on the device, where they equal 1 and u, and the unsquared route confirms they hold;
- the singular r = 0 of the idealised negative-mass interior. It does not enter at l = 0, because by Birkhoff the shell equation depends only on f_in(R).

Neither shrinks the class on which the tree applies the result. The widening from PV's wormhole to a shell with M_in < 0 is supported by Lake's general identity and re-verified. The tree never reads the result beyond linear radial order.

WHY NOT DATA-DEPENDENT. No measured quantity enters; the results are closed-form identities in m/R and beta^2.

WHY NOT WRONG. Exact sympy, z3 and an independent 40-digit numeric route all agree.

SIDE OBSERVATION, outside this grade. stability.py:76-78 restates PSP as 'all l >= 2, all compactness, all Gamma'. PSP say 'all sampled values'. That over-representation runs against M's device, not in its favour, and belongs to whichever entry audits PSP.

Recommended amendment: change the PSP later_literature read_status to READ (alphaXiv, pp. 1, 3, 4, 7, 9-11, 25). This does not change the grade.

audits.grade_evidence:
Read at source (v4 page text, pp.3, 14, 17, 19-20, 29-32). Every quotation the tree holds is verbatim and correctly paged (massform.py:1268-1297; endpoint.py:124-127, 319-327). The tree's conclusion, 'metastability is PREFERRED, not established, and it turns on the top mass' (massform.py:425; D29 at massform.py:2896-2897 and ledger.py:744-745), survives every moved datum (rederive C4-C6). The case split makes C4 independent of the metastability status (C7).

The grade is NARROWED rather than STANDS because the tree drops three hypotheses the source states:
(i) 'assuming the validity of the SM up to very high energy scales' (p.31) is not named at massform.py:414-425 or in the D29 text;
(ii) Lambda_I is carried 'in field value' (massform.py:418) without the source's 'gauge dependent ... Landau gauge' (p.20);
(iii) the lifetime sentence (massform.py:426-427) is carried without the p.30 conditions on gravity, 'unknown Planckian dynamics' and future cosmology.
None of the three moves a verdict of the tree.

Four discrepancies inside the source are recorded and not graded WRONG:
- eq.(65): printed +-1.5, against 1.74 from its stated components;
- eq.(66): printed 171.53, against 171.12 from inverting eq.(64);
- eq.(66): printed +-0.42, against 0.31 from the quadrature of its printed parts;
- 2.8 sigma one-sided is 99.74% (printed 99.8%), and p.31's 99.3% differs from p.20's 99.8%.
None of them moves the source's conclusion. The significance itself is DATA-DEPENDENT (1.9 or 5.1 sigma today, by top-mass definition), but the tree already calls it top-mass dependent and rests nothing on it.

The direction the tree needs STANDS. In the SM the high-T side is the symmetric-labelled side: z3 proves the thermal curvature coefficient is positive, and the lattice puts the crossover at 159.5 +/- 1.5 GeV. The tree's 160 GeV is 0.33 sigma from that, and no moved datum changes it.

But the tree states the result on a larger class than the source:
(i) it says '<phi> -> 0' where the source says 'approximately zero';
(ii) it says this happens AT T_c, where the source says above T_c (T_c is mid-crossover);
(iii) it says 'restores the symmetry' where the source says there is no real transition (fn.1);
(iv) it says 'un-generates the fermion masses' where the source covers tree-level vev-generated masses only;
(v) it leaves the SM-only hypothesis unnamed.

On the narrower class, FLASH_IS_A_RECONSTRUCTION_MECHANISM = False still holds on reason (a), because that objection is about direction. The grade is not WRONG: nothing is refuted. The source was not re-read this stage; the grade rests on DOCKET 65's verbatim captures.
